\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Convolution kernels with positive coefficients}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For a finite or compact Lie group #{G}, use the normalized Haar
measure, irreducibles and dimensions of the
[character expansion](fdip-007C). For convolution constructions,
consider real continuous central kernels #{k} with the separate
hypotheses
##{\begin{gathered}
k(h)>0,\qquad k(h^{-1})=k(h),\\
k(h)=\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}\dim\rho a_\rho\chi_\rho(h),
\qquad a_\rho>0,\qquad
\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}(\dim\rho)^2a_\rho<\infty.
\end{gathered}}
The last condition gives uniform absolute convergence; inversion
symmetry and reality impose the corresponding agreement of conjugate
coefficients. The convention is #{c_\rho=\dim\rho a_\rho}, not
#{c_\rho=a_\rho}. These are assumptions on a kernel, not consequences
of the Peter–Weyl theorem. In particular, pointwise positivity does
not imply the coefficient condition, as the
[circle counterexample](fdip-007J) shows.}
